Summary

This paper looks at ways to model tiny silicon sensors that work by vibrating. These sensors measure force or pressure by tracking how
their vibration frequency changes.For simple sensor designs with straightforward assumptions, basic mathematical calculations are enough
to predict how they behave. For more complicated shapes, materials that act differently in different directions, multi-layer builds,
or cases where mechanical and electrical effects interact, computer simulations that break the sensor into many small pieces work better.

The researchers tested these computer models on two types of sensors: beam-shaped ones that measure force and diaphragm-shaped ones that 
measure pressure. 

The models successfully predicted the sensors’ natural vibration frequencies, the patterns of those vibrations, how the frequencies shift under load, 
and unwanted side effects from other influences. They also helped improve the sensor shapes for clearer signals, better separation of vibration patterns, 
stronger links between electrical and mechanical parts, and improved measurement range and accuracy.The computer results matched real-world experiments,
confirming that the models are reliable.

